WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.400
Is he still in prison? Busy trying on a&nbsp;
new Batman outfit? Here's a look at why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.520
Michael Keaton's Vulture wasn't in "No Way Home."

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.680
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" is chock full&nbsp;
of villains from former Spidey films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.120
with Alfred Molina's Doc Ock&nbsp;
making a grand entrance by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.440
tearing apart a highway in pursuit of Tom&nbsp;
Holland's Spider-Man in the second act.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.000
"Hello, Peter."

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.200
Stealing the show is Willem Dafoe as Norman&nbsp;
Osborn, who plays nice before delivering a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.960
devastating blow as Green Goblin. Then there's&nbsp;
Sandman, Electro, and Lizard — one from each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.240
the five "Spider-Man" movies outside the Marvel&nbsp;
Cinematic Universe. Even Eddie Brock from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.920
"Venom" franchise pops up in a quick post-credits&nbsp;
scene, though he's far too busy getting his mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.600
blown by the existence of the MCU to take any real&nbsp;
part in the grand scheme of "No Way Home" things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.800
Oh, and as if that wasn't enough multiverse&nbsp;
goodness, the movie also gives fans the glorious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.720
return of two previous big-screen Peter Parkers,&nbsp;
portrayed by Tobey Maguire and Andrew Garfield.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.000
"Hi! How did you get out there?"

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.080
"Fire escape."

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.120
With all the returning villains, many were no&nbsp;
doubt wondering if Vulture from "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.320
Homecoming" would show up, too. In the MCU,&nbsp;
Michael Keaton's Vulture, aka Adrian Toomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.360
is Spidey's first supervillain. He cuts a&nbsp;
menacing figure in the film with his giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.320
mechanical wings, but is, of course, foiled&nbsp;
by Spiderman in the end. We last saw him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.720
in one of the "Homecoming" post-credits scenes&nbsp;
where he's in prison talking to Mac Gargan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.720
aka Scorpion, and is unwilling&nbsp;
to reveal Spider-Man's identity.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.120
"If I knew who he was, he'd already be dead."

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.880
Despite the tease in that post-credits scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.040
Vulture plays no part in "No Way Home."&nbsp;
Here's why we think he was absent.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.360
Due to Peter botching Doctor Strange's spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.360
Spider-Man villains from other universes&nbsp;
are pulled into the MCU timeline.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:56.560
"What's happening?"

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.240
"They're starting to come&nbsp;
through, and I can't stop them!"

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.840
Those who know Spider-Man's&nbsp;
true identity come through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.960
which is the opposite effect of what the spell&nbsp;
intended. However, all members of Spider-Man's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.200
rogues gallery who prominently feature in&nbsp;
the movie — as well as the figures you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.520
knocking at the proverbial door during the&nbsp;
movie's climax — have one thing in common.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.720
They're all coming through from other universes —&nbsp;
and the only ones who actually make it through are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.680
characters who have already been in different&nbsp;
"Spider-Man" movies. Dafoe's Green Goblin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.960
Molina's Doc Ock, and Church's Sandman all&nbsp;
hail from Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man" trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.440
while Rhys Ifans' Lizard and Jamie Foxx's&nbsp;
Electro both come from Marc Webb's "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.560
Amazing Spider-Man" movies, and Hardy's Venom&nbsp;
is his own thing. The movie also seems to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.360
for one iconic villain per movie, which rules&nbsp;
out baddies like James Franco's New Goblin.

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:53.920
"No. He despised you. You were&nbsp;
an embarrassment to him."

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.320
Sure, Keaton's Vulture knows Spider-Man's&nbsp;
identity, but since the movie's villains are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.000
specifically from different universes, that pretty&nbsp;
much rules Mr. Toomes out. After all, Vulture is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.160
already firmly entrenched in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe, so he doesn't really fit the villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.880
requirements to play a big role in "No Way Home."&nbsp;
Besides, even if he did, there's the fact that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.840
far as viewers know, Vulture is still in prison&nbsp;
after the events of "Spider-Man: Homecoming."

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.760
As "Spider-Man: No Way Home" readily&nbsp;
and repeatedly proves, you can never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.080
count a good Spider-Man villain out just&nbsp;
because they've been defeated in one movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.160
Besides, Michael Keaton's impending&nbsp;
return as Batman implies that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.880
acting legend is still perfectly willing to&nbsp;
operate in the world of superheroes. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.280
it might very well be that one of the reasons you&nbsp;
didn't see Keaton's flying villain in "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.440
No Way Home" is that he's already set&nbsp;
to feature in another upcoming project.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.040
A recent trailer revealed that some&nbsp;
version of Vulture will likely be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.960
in Sony's upcoming "Morbius" – at&nbsp;
least, judging by the fact that Keaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.640
appears as a man who sure seems to be Adrian&nbsp;
Toomes, talking to the titular Dr. Morbius.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.680
"Hey, uh, Doctor Michael? You&nbsp;
and I should stay in touch."

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.360
According to The Hollywood Reporter, Keaton&nbsp;
is indeed playing Vulture in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.600
even though it appears that "Morbius"&nbsp;
doesn't take place in the MCU. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.560
it exists in the same universe as "Venom,"&nbsp;
which is certainly different from the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.240
going by the fact that the "Venom:&nbsp;
Let There Be Carnage" and "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.520
No Way Home" post-credits scenes depict Eddie&nbsp;
Brock being transported between the two universes.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.440
As such, it certainly seems that you will&nbsp;
see Keaton's Vulture sooner rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.440
later — only, it's in a different corner of&nbsp;
the multiverse than you might have expected.

